MPQS / SIQS in plain JavaScript
==============================

Open MPQS.html in a current browser (Chrome, Edge, Firefox or Safari with
BigInt, Web Workers and Web Crypto support). No build, package installation,
network service, or external library is required. All computation is local.
The scripts and stylesheet must remain alongside MPQS.html.

Enter a positive whole decimal number of at most 110 digits and click
Factorize, or press Enter. Surrounding whitespace and leading zeros are
accepted; internal whitespace, signs, separators and scientific notation are
not. Leading zeros count toward the input limit. Overlength input is rejected,
never silently truncated. Factorize is disabled until the syntax is valid.

Threads defaults to 1. Choose a whole number from 1 through 32 before starting.
The value is the total number of computation workers, including the worker
that coordinates the search. It is locked during a calculation. Invalid or
empty thread counts disable Factorize. Clear preserves the Threads value.
Threads controls parallel SQUFOF multipliers, Brent-Rho attempts, ECM curves, sieving and the sparse matrix
products during block Lanczos. More threads can help larger composites; worker startup,
communication, and CPU/cache contention mean that more threads are not always
faster.

ECM Pretesting is checked by default. Uncheck it to skip the ECM prepass.
It is locked during a calculation, and Clear preserves its setting along
with Threads. Enabled ECM runs after Brent-Rho and before MPQS/SIQS, only
for remaining composite cofactors above 45 digits.

Factoring starts with trial division by every prime up to 1000, removing all
copies of each small factor. Only the remaining cofactor needs primality
testing and, if composite, perfect-power detection. Remaining composites
with at most 24 actual decimal digits are fully factored using SQUFOF,
recursively splitting each divisor until all factors pass primality testing.
The size check is repeated after each split; leading zeroes do not count.
Cofactors of 25 digits or more skip this SQUFOF path and use the existing
Brent-Rho/ECM/MPQS pipeline. Inputs completely
factored by trial division finish without Miller-Rabin or MPQS. Prime inputs
are still reported as such, with no factor list.

When trial division finds factors and leaves a cofactor, those factors appear
in Factors immediately, before work on the cofactor begins. The help text
marks them as a partial result while the remaining cofactor is unresolved.
Verified factors recovered by SQUFOF, Brent-Rho or ECM also appear as partial results while
the remaining cofactor is unresolved. Raw composite divisors are recursively
factored before appearing in this field.
When factoring finishes, the complete sorted list replaces the partial list.
Clear or editing the input removes the partial result and its label.

Factors is read-only. Repeated factors are displayed as powers; for example,
360 becomes 2^3 x 3^2 x 5 (the page uses a multiplication symbol).
Clear empties Composite and Factors and the status counters, preserves Threads and ECM Pretesting,
and immediately terminates every worker in the calculation.
Composite and Factors have horizontal scrolling for long values. On
small screens their containers also scroll, preserving a 50-character field.

The bottom status area uses an msieve-style relation line:
  2,903 relations (2,537 full + 366 combined from 97,740 partial), need 57,104

Before sieving, the Brent-Rho status shows the cofactor size, thread count,
total attempts and polynomial evaluations, and percentage of the shared
active-worker time allowance consumed.
The relation counters are zero during this stage; the relation bar continues
to describe sieving only.
For small cofactors, the SQUFOF status instead shows cofactor size, thread
count, multipliers tried and total form iterations across the worker pool.
There is no estimated percentage for this search.
During ECM, status shows preparation/stage 1/stage 2, the cofactor size,
thread count, completed curves versus the shared planned count, achieved
versus target t-depth, and the current B1/B2 bounds. A curve that finds a
factor early is not credited as a fully completed curve. Relation counts
stay zero during ECM; the relation progress bar remains specific to sieving.

These example values illustrate the format; actual counters update live:
  relations: all usable relations in the current matrix (full + combined).
  full: directly smooth relations, without a remaining large factor.
  combined: relations formed by matching partials or closing cycles in the
    double-large-prime graph, including relations from different workers.
  partial: all partials collected in this cofactor search, including those
    already combined or evicted from the cache, not just unmatched partials.
  need: estimated factor-base columns (including sign), plus 16
    for compact MPQS, a margin of 5% (at least 16) for smaller SIQS searches,
    or 96 extra relations for large SIQS searches.
    This is a heuristic target, not an exact remaining count or percentage.
    Factoring can finish sooner or require more relations.
  Relation collection bar: full + combined divided by the estimated target,
    capped at 100%, with a percentage alongside it. An empty bar and a dash
    mean that no estimate is available yet. Reaching 100% does not guarantee
    that factoring is finished, and an early result can leave it below 100%.

For large searches the status then shows Filtering matrix, Block Lanczos,
and Extracting factors. During matrix work it displays the matrix dimensions
instead of the sieve's polynomial/thread counts. Lanczos also reports its
iteration, dimensions processed, and the number of participating threads.
The relation bar still describes relation
collection, not linear-algebra progress. If dependencies are insufficient or
produce only trivial GCDs, sieving resumes with a larger relation target.

The counters describe the current cofactor search, not a cumulative total
across recursive factoring. They are zero when small-factor removal, SQUFOF, Brent-Rho, ECM
or perfect powers finish the job without MPQS. A fresh cofactor resets all counters.
Recycling a matrix resets usable/full/combined counts; the total partial count
continues for that cofactor. Final counts remain visible until editing or Clear.
The monospace relation line scrolls horizontally when it does not fit.
With multiple threads, counts and progress cover the shared relation collector,
not separate per-worker targets. The status message shows the active thread
count during sieving (small cofactors use one worker).

Algorithm
---------
The implementation uses msieve-1.36/common/smallfact/tinyqs.c and the full
SIQS code in msieve-1.36/mpqs/ as guides. Those sources were placed in the
public domain by Jason Papadopoulos. The large-input improvements also use
the local msieve/mpqs/ and msieve/common/smallfact/squfof.c as guides.
It uses:
  - trial division through 1000 before primality testing;
  - 30-round Miller-Rabin with early composite exit and unbiased, cryptographic
    rejection-sampled random bases from 2 through n-2;
  - perfect-power detection for remaining composite cofactors;
  - BigInt SQUFOF for composite cofactors with at most 24 digits;
  - bounded Brent-Rho attempts for composite cofactors with at least 40 digits;
  - optional two-stage ECM with YAFU's normal pretesting target above 45 digits;
  - a quadratic-residue factor base and Tonelli-Shanks modular square roots;
  - multiple polynomials Q(x) = A*x^2 + 2*B*x + C, with B^2=k*n mod A;
  - a Knuth-Schroeppel-style multiplier score for larger inputs;
  - a logarithmic sieve including prime powers, followed by exact division;
  - single-large-prime matching and, from 282 bits, double-large-prime cycles;
  - bounded SQUFOF splitting of small residuals, with exact split checks;
  - singleton deletion, weight-two merging and heavy-column pruning;
  - 32-way block Lanczos for large matrices, packed Gaussian elimination for
    smaller searches, all over GF(2);
  - square congruences and GCD(X +/- Y, n), rejecting trivial dependencies;
  - recursive factoring, sorted output, and an exact final product check.

The small-composite SQUFOF path follows
msieve-1.36/common/smallfact/squfof.c. It races the original sixteen square-free
multipliers, saves small denominators to reject improper square forms, and
traverses the inverse square-root form to recover a divisor. Every divisor is
checked by exact GCD/division against the original cofactor.

All scaled numbers, forms, square roots and GCDs use BigInt integer arithmetic.
Inputs up to 24 digits can exceed JavaScript Number's exact range, and multipliers
can enlarge them further. Number is used only for counters, indexing and
elapsed time. No floating-point square roots or divisions choose factors.

This is the complete factoring path for eligible small composites, without a
1% time allowance or a fallback to MPQS. Failed multipliers are replaced by
new square-free multipliers formed from the original set and additional odd
primes. Forward periods without a proper square and overflowing saved-form
queues are retired. Each worker receives distinct multipliers and races its
assigned forms in blocks of 300 even/odd pairs. Short resumable batches keep
the worker protocol responsive. Finding a proper divisor stops new batches;
existing batches finish before the helpers are released for the next cofactor.
Clear terminates all workers immediately and preserves the Threads value.
As with other factoring methods, completion time varies with the number.

The existing bounded Number SQUFOF routine used to split double-large-prime
sieve residuals remains separate. Its inputs are below 2^52 (at most 16
decimal digits), so it also stays within the 24-digit limit.

Brent-Rho follows BrentRho/BrentRho.c: the walk x <- (x*x+c) mod n doubles
its cycle length, batches up to 128 differences per GCD, and replays a batch
one step at a time when its GCD is n. Failed or exhausted walks restart with
new seeds/constants (2,1), (3,2), ... for at most max(8, Threads) attempts
across the entire pool. Each seed/constant pair belongs to just one worker.
A divisor must be strictly between 1 and n and divide n exactly.

Only composite cofactors of 40 or more actual decimal digits enter Rho.
Leading zeroes do not count, and the threshold applies after trial division
and at every recursive split. Probable primes and perfect powers are handled
first. Composites of 25 through 39 digits go directly to MPQS/SIQS; smaller
ones use SQUFOF.

The Rho allowance targets 1% of estimated remaining factoring time. It uses a
rough size model based on the existing local timing records, not a new
benchmark or a guarantee of actual factoring time. The model interpolates
logarithmically between about 4 seconds at 52 digits on one worker and a
6,700 worker-second proxy at 86 digits (the historical 838-second, eight-worker
run). With d digits and t selected Threads:

  estimated milliseconds = 4000 * exp(log(6700/4) * (d - 52) / 34) / t
  Rho milliseconds       = min(30000, 0.01 * estimated milliseconds)

Examples of the initial total Rho allowance (shared, not per worker):
  Digits       1 thread       8 threads
     40          2.9 ms           0.36 ms
     50         25.8 ms           3.23 ms
     60        229.4 ms          28.68 ms
     70          2.04 s           0.25 s
     80         18.08 s           2.26 s
     86         30.00 s           8.38 s
    110         30.00 s          30.00 s

Actual costs vary with the factors, hardware and worker scaling; the model
predates the latest matrix improvements. Rho can finish early, so its actual
share can be much smaller than 1%. The existing size model supplies one total
allowance, divided equally among the selected workers. Time spent actively
advancing each walk is added across workers; waiting for messages or other
workers does not consume it. Elapsed wall time is recorded separately.
The initial allowance is also shared across all recursive Rho searches, each
additionally limited by its own cofactor's estimate. A total cap of 20 million
polynomial evaluations is partitioned across workers in the same way.
Increasing Threads never grants each worker the full allowance.

Walks preserve their state between short batches, yielding after about 4 ms
or 64 checkpoints. Time is checked at most every 128 evaluations and on every
replay step; one short batch/GCD or OS scheduling can overrun a worker's
deadline. These small overruns are included in the consumed allowance.
When any worker finds a divisor, no further batches are scheduled. Already
running batches finish, then helpers release their Rho state and can be
reused for a recursive cofactor, sieving or block Lanczos. Replies carry
job/request identifiers so late messages cannot contaminate another search.
Exhausting the allowance never means prime or failure: factoring continues
with MPQS/SIQS. Clear still terminates the entire worker pool.

ECM pretesting
--------------
The ECM prepass follows the normal size-based scheduling strategy in the
local yafu/factor/autofactor.c, with Suyama curves as in
yafu/factor/avx-ecm/avxecm.c. Those sources are public domain.
For d remaining decimal digits, the target is t(4*d/13). Up to 45 digits,
the normal plan skips ECM. Prime/probable-prime inputs and perfect powers
take their existing paths first. This adds ECM to the browser's pipeline;
it does not add YAFU's Fermat, p-1, p+1, SNFS or NFS stages.

ECM uses exact BigInt Montgomery x:z point arithmetic, Suyama curve
parameterization, and multiplication by the largest power of each prime
through B1. Stage 2 uses baby/giant steps and prime pairing q=kD +/- r,
with B2=100*B1. Projective cross-products are accumulated for batched GCDs;
if a batch GCD equals the input, its individual terms are checked to recover
factors hidden by batching. All returned divisors are checked by exact
division. Composite divisors are recursively factored before being shown.

The bound ladder for this page's <=110-digit inputs is:
  Target level       B1             B2
       t15           2,000          200,000
       t20          11,000        1,100,000
       t25          50,000        5,000,000
       t30         250,000       25,000,000
       t35       1,000,000      100,000,000

Scheduling uses YAFU's avx_ecm_data table, which estimates Suyama curves with
power-1 stage 2 and B2=100*B1, matching this implementation's bounds.
Each level's diagonal curve estimate supplies its maximum batch, with the
last batch reduced to reach the target after crediting earlier levels.
These counts differ from GMP-ECM's default-B2 schedule. They are heuristic
factor-search depths, not proofs that smaller factors are absent, runtime
predictions, or guarantees of matching native YAFU's performance.

For an unchanged 86-digit composite the initial plan is 564 curves:
32 at B1=2000, 98 at 11000, 281 at 50000 and 153 at 250000, targeting t26.46.
The same total applies to every Threads setting. Workers get disjoint curve
indices from a random starting sigma. Completed curves are credited to
descendant cofactors, whose smaller digit counts can require less work.
When the plan is exhausted without a factor, MPQS/SIQS resumes normally.

The coordinator also computes curves, so Threads is the total computation
worker count. Jobs preserve state across roughly 4-ms/128-step batches.
One batch per helper is in flight; finding a factor stops new waves and
drains the current wave before helpers are released. Job/request IDs isolate
late messages. Clear terminates the entire pool immediately.

Stage-2 primes are generated with a segmented sieve and stored as compact
prime-pair masks. Each worker caches only the most recent bound's table,
not every individual prime through B2. No native code, WebAssembly, SharedArrayBuffer,
cross-origin isolation, or external factoring service is used.

The programmatic factor(value, progress, options), factorParallel(value,
progress, ports, options), and startFactorization(value, threads, callback,
options) APIs accept { ecmPretesting: true }. Omission preserves their
previous behavior; the page explicitly passes the checkbox value.

Remaining composites of 25 digits use the compact prime-square MPQS path
(A=q^2). Larger composites use self-initializing MPQS (SIQS): A is a product
of distinct factor-base primes near sqrt(2*k*n)/halfInterval. CRT components
generate up to 2048 related B values per A. Gray-code updates reuse modular
inverses and update sieve roots with bounded additions/subtractions.

Parallel SIQS assigns different A families to workers from one central
selector. Each worker reuses its own sieve buffers and roots. Raw full and
partial relations are sent directly to the coordinator through MessagePorts;
the coordinator combines partials across all workers and performs the
linear algebra. The coordinator also sieves in bounded batches, yielding
between batches to process helper results. Helpers wait for acknowledgement
before producing the next batch, bounding the amount of queued data.
Search identifiers discard any late results from a previous cofactor.
During batch matrix solving, sieving pauses and the same helpers perform
Lanczos matrix products. Each helper has at most one unacknowledged sieve
batch; these batches are processed if sieving resumes. Matrix replies use a
separate request path so paused sieve work cannot block the solver. Helpers
release their matrix slices before resuming their existing sieve jobs.

The page owns the entire worker pool. Completion, Clear, page exit, or an
error terminates all workers. No SharedArrayBuffer, cross-origin isolation,
WebAssembly, external library, or server-side computation is needed.
Trial division, primality testing, perfect-power detection, and the compact
MPQS path (cofactors of 25 digits) run on the coordinator only.

The SIQS factor-base size is interpolated from Msieve's bit-length parameter
table. Below 240 bits the cap is 12,000 primes; larger inputs can use up to
100,000. The 86-digit benchmark uses 55,333 primes. Half-intervals are 16,384,
32,768, 65,536 or 262,144, depending on size. The single-large-prime cutoff L
is 40 or 80 times the largest factor-base prime. At 282 bits and above,
residuals up to L * floor(L^0.8), capped below 2^52, can be split into two
bounded factors. A base-2 Fermat filter rejects likely prime residuals above
L before SQUFOF. This filter cannot accept an invalid relation: every split
is checked by exact BigInt division. An unsuccessful split is discarded.

Double-large-prime relations are edges in a shared spanning forest. An edge
closing a cycle produces a usable relation whose large factors occur an even
number of times. Tree edges remain available to subsequent independent
cycles, so combined relations can share partials. A deduplication set rejects
repeated polynomial relations. Packed exponent arrays reduce storage and
worker-message size. The former 50,000-entry cache limit does not apply to
this graph.

SIQS uses eighth-bit logarithmic weights in a reusable Uint16Array. Small
prime powers have precomputed modular roots. The densest updates (powers of
2 through 32, powers of 3 through 9, and 5) form a 1440-entry repeating pattern,
expanded with typed-array copies. The remaining sieve loops are unrolled.
A conservative coarse cutoff skips most logarithm calls; exact polynomial
evaluation and trial division still validate every accepted relation. Large
searches use flat typed arrays for roots, weights and Gray-code shifts.
Candidate factoring checks small-prime residues directly and re-sieves
large-prime hits, avoiding a complete factor-base scan for every candidate.

Small SIQS searches use online Gaussian elimination with packed Uint32Array
parity and dependency vectors, clz32 pivot selection and bounded XOR loops.
Large searches (from 240 bits) collect the estimated relation target first.
Their matrix uses one column per usable relation and one row per exponent
parity constraint, including the sign. Filtering repeatedly:
  - removes columns containing singleton rows, including resulting cascades;
  - merges pairs hitting weight-two rows, while bounding fill-in;
  - extracts zero-column dependencies and removes empty rows;
  - prunes the heaviest excess columns, retaining 64 surplus columns;
  - orders rows by decreasing weight and keeps the mapping back to every
    original relation.

The filtered matrix is solved with Montgomery block Lanczos, following
msieve/common/lanczos. Blocks contain 32 vectors, using Uint32Array XORs,
byte-table block products, and sparse row/column layouts. The 16 densest rows
are temporarily omitted on matrices larger than 64 rows and restored during
the final nullspace calculation. The three-block Lanczos recurrence handles
singular blocks with generalized inverses; independent starting blocks are
retried on failure. A direct inner product with the initial block replaces
msieve's auxiliary small-matrix recurrence.

With Threads greater than 1, every Lanczos iteration distributes both sparse
products, B*v and transpose(B)*(B*v), across the selected worker pool,
including the coordinator. Rows and columns are partitioned by nonzero count
to balance the work. Each helper receives its sparse slices once per solve;
vector blocks are copied to helpers and product slices are transferred back.
The coordinator assembles the exact GF(2) result before the next recurrence
step. Threads=1 uses the same recurrence with synchronous matrix products.
This is one cooperative solve, rather than independent random solver races.

Filtering, the small block recurrences, final nullspace combination,
dependency checks and BigInt GCD extraction remain on the coordinator.
Message overhead can outweigh the benefit on small matrices; increasing
Threads is not a guarantee of a speedup. Matrix storage on helpers is
partitioned, rather than a complete matrix copy per worker. Each helper also
temporarily receives the current vector block.

Candidate dependencies of B'B are combined into actual dependencies of B.
Each dependency must be nonzero and pass an exact parity check against the
full filtered matrix, then against the original matrix after expanding the
filtering history. Only then is it used for BigInt square-congruence/GCD
extraction. Four unsuccessful starts, insufficient filtered rank, or only
trivial GCDs cause more relation collection; the original relations remain
available. No approximate floating-point solver or Gaussian fallback is used
for the large matrix.

For each relation, (A*x+B)^2 = A*Q(x) modulo n. Compact MPQS carries q as a
known square-root factor. SIQS adds the prime factors of A to each relation's
exponents. Matched partials and graph cycles carry their paired large factors
in the square root.
Negative Q values use a sign column. GCD extraction uses the original cofactor
and rejects trivial dependencies. No factor for a benchmark input is built in.

Cofactor, polynomial and factor arithmetic uses BigInt. Sieve residues and
modular inverses use exact Number arithmetic with moduli below 2^26, so
products of two residues are below 2^52. Packed parity vectors use 32-bit
integer operations. Approximate floating-point polynomial values only select
sieve candidates; exact division and product checks validate the result.

Numbers passing Miller-Rabin are probable primes, not primality certificates.
For an odd composite, 30 independent uniform rounds have a false-prime
probability at most 4^-30. Prime inputs are reported without attempting MPQS.

Limits
------
110 digits is an input limit, NOT a promise of practical factoring time.
Balanced semiprimes near that size are not practical for this browser MPQS.
Inputs of that length with small factors or perfect powers may finish quickly.
Performance depends heavily on factor sizes, the number and the computer.

There is no fixed time limit. Work runs in Web Workers so Clear remains
responsive. The single-large-prime matching cache is limited to 50,000 entries.
Smaller searches recycle the matrix after factor-base size + 256 accepted
relations if no nontrivial dependency has succeeded. Large searches retain
the matrix and the double-prime forest; their memory grows with collected
relations. An 86-digit job with several workers can need multiple gigabytes.
Each extra thread also has its own factor-base/sieve buffers. Browser memory limits can prevent
completion of larger jobs. Relations are kept in RAM only.

Files and tests
---------------
MPQS.html / styles.css  : page and presentation
app.js                 : validation, worker lifecycle and status display
mpqs.js                : shared factoring engine and Blob Worker source
matrix.js              : filtering, block Lanczos and dependency verification
ecm.js                 : two-stage BigInt ECM, normal pretesting plans and curve workers


Directly opening MPQS.html works.
